# Abstract draft

This is a working draft. It is not submitted copy. Riff here, then keep `data/talk.json` in sync. `abstractStatus` stays `draft` until Joel says otherwise.

Do not treat this page as Joel's final voice. Invent nothing he did not say. Tighten from sourced theses only.

## Current draft

A swarm of coding agents will take the shortest path. If that path is sludge, you get sludge at scale. This talk zooms in on the floor those minions stand on: a TypeScript CLI scaffold with written law, an enforceable fence, and Effect so bad input fails loud. Cascadia covered why swarms need a harness. The AIE workshop taught the reliability floor. JoelClaw is what happens when you stop renting the shape of an agent and build the claw yourself. Twenty-five minutes. You leave with a clone command.

## What it has to do

- Keep the title **The Claw: Minions of Toil**
- Fit a 25-minute general web-dev room
- Name swarms without remounting Cascadia
- Name the floor (`ts-cli-template` / this clone) without teaching eight workshop lessons
- Let claw / JoelClaw / minions register without dumping private course design
- End on a concrete takeaway: clone the floor

## Sourced lines we can steal from

- Dillon Mulroy, 2026-08-19: "effect make typescript good. typescript hard to make good. effect make good typescript easy."
- Cascadia riff: more agents was not the hard part; the harness owns the scary parts.
- AIE Lesson 01/02: write the stops first; Effect Schema; fail loud; save a receipt.
- `ts-cli-template` VISION: agents take the shortest path; cheap path = honest path.
- JoelClaw lore: stop renting the shape of an agent; own the harness.
- The Claw course pitch (public title only): minions of toil; game mechanics are agent architecture. Do not paste the private course bible.

